  "notes": [
    "a deployed manifest carries a signature, an RFC 3161 timestamp token and, where revocation is stapled, an OCSP response",
    "each of the three brings its own certificate chain, so the certificate term is paid two or three times",
    "x5chain and the timestamp token each carry signer and intermediate; roots are the validator's trust anchors and are not carried",
    "OCSP responses are real objects signed under the measured algorithm, in two shapes: signed by the issuing CA (no certificate inside) and by a delegated responder whose certificate travels in the response; manifest_full_bytes uses the CA-signed shape",
    "signature-only and timestamped manifests are what deployments ship; the OCSP configuration is modelled, since no corpus signature staples one",
    "timestamp tokens are real RFC 3161 tokens built by pq_tst.py: CMS SignedData, TSTInfo, content-type, message-digest and signing-certificate-v2 attributes; TSA hierarchy of three with the timeStamping EKU, of which signer and intermediate travel; written to tst/<alg>.tst",
    "timestamp_token_bytes_model is the earlier model, signature + chain + 500 B",
    "the certificate term dominates in every configuration, so the structural estimate does not affect the conclusion",
    "merged from 10 independent runs (fresh processes and keys, random experiment and configuration order); median_ms is the median of per-run medians, ci95_ms a 95% bootstrap interval over runs",
    "cose_wrap_corrected (34n): COSE wrapping taken from e1_postquantum instead of a fixed 120 B; manifest_full_bytes now uses the delegated OCSP response (src/fix_timestamp_34n.py)"
  ],
